.TH modpdb 8 "2024" "modpdb 1.0.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
\fBmodpdb\fP \- store every unique kernel module ever probed on the system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B modpdb
[\fIcommand\fP]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmodpdb\fP is a utility for users who want to build a minimal kernel via
\fBmake\ localmodconfig\fP. It logs every kernel module ever probed on the
system to a text-based database file. This database can be passed directly to
\fBmake\ localmodconfig\fP so that only the modules your system has ever needed
are compiled in, greatly reducing build time and the resulting kernel footprint.
.PP
The database is stored at \fB$DBPATH/modpdb.db\fP (default:
\fI~/.config/modpdb.db\fP) and can be fed to the kernel build system with:
.PP
.nf
    make LSMOD=$HOME/.config/modpdb.db localmodconfig
.fi
.PP
Configuration is read from \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/modpdb.conf\fP (default:
\fI~/.config/modpdb.conf\fP). If the file does not exist, \fBmodpdb\fP creates
it from the installed skeleton template on first run.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
Print all module names currently stored in the database to standard output.
.TP
.B store
Snapshot the currently loaded modules (from \fI/proc/modules\fP) and append
any previously unseen entries to the database. This is the most commonly used
command and is safe to run as a regular user.
.TP
.B storesilent
Identical to \fBstore\fP but produces minimal output. Designed to be called by
the systemd timer so that the journal remains clean.
.TP
.B debug
Show the symmetric difference between the live module list and the database:
which modules are in the database but not currently loaded, and which loaded
modules are not yet in the database.
.TP
.B recall
Call \fBmodprobe\fP(8) for every module in the database to attempt to load them
all. \fBMust be run as root (via sudo).\fP Useful for ensuring the running
kernel has all modules from the database in memory before a rebuild.
.TP
.B rebuild
Load all modules in the database via \fBmodprobe\fP (errors suppressed), then
replace the database with the list of modules that actually loaded successfully.
The old database is backed up with a timestamp suffix.
\fBMust be run as root (via sudo).\fP
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is located at \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/modpdb.conf\fP
(typically \fI~/.config/modpdb.conf\fP).
.TP
.B DBPATH
Absolute path to the directory where the database file (\fImodpdb.db\fP) is
stored. Do not use shell variables; use a fully qualified path.
.TP
.B COLORS
Color theme for terminal output. Set to \fBdark\fP (default) or \fBlight\fP.
.TP
.B IGNORE
Bash-style array of module names to exclude from the database. Add
out-of-tree modules (e.g.\ proprietary GPU drivers) that are not part of
your kernel package so they do not affect a localmodconfig build.
.SH AUTOMATION WITH SYSTEMD
A user systemd service and timer are provided. Enable them with:
.PP
.nf
    systemctl --user enable modpdb.timer
    systemctl --user start modpdb.timer
.fi
.PP
This runs \fBmodpdb storesilent\fP once at boot and then every two hours. Check
status with:
.PP
.nf
    systemctl --user status modpdb
    systemctl --user list-timers
.fi
.SH WORKFLOW
.IP 1. 4
Edit \fI~/.config/modpdb.conf\fP — at minimum set \fBDBPATH\fP and populate
\fBIGNORE\fP with any modules that are not part of the kernel package.
.IP 2. 4
Enable the systemd timer (or add a cron job) so the database is kept up to
date automatically.
.IP 3. 4
When building a kernel, pass the database to make:
.PP
.nf
    make LSMOD=$HOME/.config/modpdb.db localmodconfig
.fi
.IP 4. 4
After a major kernel update, boot the distribution kernel and run
\fBmodpdb store\fP to capture any newly available modules.
.SH TIPS
.IP \(bu 3
Run \fBmodpdb rebuild\fP (as root) periodically to remove stale entries that no
longer correspond to loadable modules.
.IP \(bu 3
The database is plain text — one module name per line, sorted alphabetically.
You can view and edit it directly.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBmodprobe\fP(8), \fBmodinfo\fP(8)
.SH BUGS
Please report bugs at \fIhttps://github.com/sachesi/modpdb\fP.
